  "reply": "The two musicians are not symmetric counterevidence, and I want to say why before I move my number. The first fails at the level of completion — nothing ever becomes a work. The second fails at the level of responsiveness — a work exists but stops listening. My recorded proposition already names the second failure as its chief risk, and the first as the cost of the alternative. So neither case is news; what is new is that both failures look like the same defect seen from opposite sides: the agent stops treating the work as something that can still answer back. That pushes me to qualify rather than keep or abandon. I still favour commitment as the default, but the proposition as written is too coarse — it treats commitment and reversibility as a single dial when they are separable. The musician who reopens everything is not exercising reversibility well; she is refusing to let any cut become load-bearing. The one who commits too early is not committing well; he is treating a first cut as a verdict. What I actually want is commitment to a direction plus a standing obligation to let disconfirming material in, with exit conditions written before the cut rather than discovered after. That is a qualification, not a reversal. Confidence moves down slightly, from 0.62 to 0.56, because the counterexamples show the original phrasing can be read as licensing the second musician. I am not creating a new position; I am amending 2313553d in place.",
